Body:
I do not know what else to say or add to this, the tool returns a response that has this exact shape:
```json
{
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "image",
      "data": "iVBOR...",
      "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
    }
  ]
}
```
This use to work fine, now it no longer works, it works fine in other agents and it even works fine in the default llama.cpp chat UI. If this is not strictly not part of the MCP spec your still breaking existing workflows and adding nothing of value by being overly strict. Being technically correct is not the same as actually being compatible with what people call "MCP".

I understand this is an opensource project and that you owe me nothing. Should I wait for a fix or is that not gonna happen? I'm fine with pinning and rolling back to an older version, thats a valid solution, I just want to know what the future holds so I can plan for it.

Well that's completely different from what the other person reported, they were saying this issue:
```
{
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "image",
      "data": "iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAEAAAABCAIAAACQd1PeAAAADElEQVR4nGP4z8AAAAMBAQDJ/pLvAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC",
      "mimeType": "image/png"
    }
  ],
  "structuredContent": { "ok": true },
  "isError": false
}
```

where it has content and structuredContent and they are different

ex:
```
  "provider": {
    "custom": {
      "models": {
        "qwen": {
          "modalities": {
            "input": ["text", "image"],
            "output": ["text"],
          },
        },
      },
    },
  },
```

@rekram1-node 
I took my time reading through the MCP specification and the reference server implementations. And it looks like you're right - `content` and `structuredContent` do appear together, but despite that, there's no explicit specification anywhere on how they should be processed together.  
I'm reimplementing MCP (as you probably guessed, it was a custom MCP) right now to drop `structuredContent` completely. I'll let you know if it will fix the issue.
